 To accomplish this task, you will need to use the `chrome-remote-interface` library to control Chrome and `chrome-har` to generate the HAR file. Below is a basic example of how you can achieve this. This example assumes you have Node.js installed on your system.

First, ensure you have the necessary packages installed:

```bash
npm install chrome-remote-interface chrome-har
```

Here's a simple script that uses `chrome-remote-interface` to start a Chrome browser instance, navigate to a URL, and then use `chrome-har` to generate a HAR file after the session is complete:

```javascript
const { Remote } = require('chrome-remote-interface');
const { HAR } = require('chrome-har');

// Create a new instance of the Chrome browser
const chrome = new Remote();

// Wait for the browser to be ready
chrome.on('Browser', async (browser) => {
  // Create a new tab
  const tab = await browser.tabs.create({ url: 'https://www.example.com' });

  // Wait for the tab to be ready
  chrome.on('Page', async (page) => {
    // Wait for the page to load
    await page.loadEventFired();

    // Wait for 5 seconds to allow the page to load fully
    await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 5000));

    // Generate the HAR file
    const har = new HAR();
    await har.start();
    await har.stop();

    // Save the HAR file
    const harFile = har.getHAR();
    const fs = require('fs');
    fs.writeFileSync('example.har', JSON.stringify(harFile, null, 2));

    // Close the tab and browser
    await tab.close();
    await browser.close();
  });
});
```

This script will create a new Chrome browser instance, navigate to `https://www.example.com`, wait for the page to load, generate a HAR file, and then save it to a file named `example.har` in the current working directory.

